{
  "slug": "existential-predicate",
  "title": "Existential Predicate",
  "definition": "A [term:predicate] that asserts the existence of a referent, typically in relation to a locative or temporal ground (∃x [LOC/TEMP]). The subject NP is typically indefinite. Existential predicates often employ a dedicated verb or invariant particle distinct from the regular [term:copula], and frequently use special negation (a 'lack' or 'absence' verb). They overlap structurally with [term:locative-predicate]s and [term:presentational-construction]s, and often grammaticalize into [term:possessive-predicate] constructions.",
  "relatedTerms": ["locative-predicate", "possessive-predicate", "presentational-construction", "copula", "predicate"],
  "examples": [
